CLAUDE.md:230:- **In place, report-only** (phase 3, since 2026-10-27): `apps/bacteria-game-remote/e2e/perf.spec.ts` (Chromium only) plays up to 20 s (at least 8) of a campaign match against the computer on the CPU simulation (`?sim=cpu`), with `Math.random` pinned to a seed by an init script, and reports the frame interval (p50, p95, max), long tasks and the engine's time per tick, each also as a ratio to the calibration loop run in the page. The tick time comes from the game's probe (the engine's `state/match/perf-probe.ts`): the spec puts `window.__bacteriaPerfProbe` on the page before it loads, and `GameStateService.advance` times each tick only then; the game never creates it. The table goes to the log (and the job summary where `GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY` is set), the numbers into the attachment `frame-time.json`; it fails only when nothing was measured. On SwiftShader the frames take about 67 ms and a tick about 2.5 ms: the painting, not the engine, sets the pace there.
CLAUDE.md:231:- **In place, report-only** (phase 4, since 2026-10-27): Lighthouse CI (`@lhci/cli`) as a `lighthouse` target on `portfolio` (every prerendered route, listed in its `lighthouse.json`, which `app.component.spec.ts` keeps equal to `app.routes.ts`), `angular-examples` (`/`) and `rollapolla-analog` (`/`, `/polls`), each after its `build`, cached, alone on its worker. `tools/perf/lighthouse-run.mjs <app>` runs `lhci autorun` on Playwright's Chrome (`CHROME_PATH` wins where set), with the app's `apps/<app>/lighthouse.json` (what to serve, which pages) and the shared settings it holds itself: 3 runs per page, mobile defaults, warnings only (LCP 2.5 s, TBT 200 ms, CLS 0.1, 1.6 MB), uploaded to temporary public storage in CI (links in the log), `.lighthouseci/` elsewhere. rollapolla's build is Nitro's `firebase` preset, which listens nowhere: `tools/perf/serve-firebase-function.mjs` serves its public folder and hands every other path to the exported function, as Hosting does; its SSR reads the real project's public polls. The public replay pages of the game (a Cloud Function behind Hosting) are not measured yet.
CLAUDE.md:233:  - A failing gate is fixed in the code, not by raising the limit. Raise a budget or baseline only for growth you want, in its own `perf(<project>): ...` commit, with what grew, by how much and why in the PR body. Lower it when a gate reports a gain.
